    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity 5yr CAGR 13.3% diverges >30% from EPS 5yr CAGR 8.5%, and equity is now shrinking ($3.70B FY2025 -> $3.04B Q2-2026) as $755M of H1-2026 buybacks are funded with convertible debt. The 5yr revenue CAGR (15.9%) is inflated by the NIC acquisition (2021). Book value is not a usable growth anchor for this business.",
    "notes": "Owner-FCF series (OCF - capex - capitalized software - SBC - withholding taxes - interest income when net cash material): FY2020 $246.3M, FY2022 $201.2M, FY2024 $400.2M, FY2025 $388.3M. The 5yr owner-FCF CAGR (9.5%) is well below the 5yr reported-FCF CAGR (13.7%): SBC + withholding rose from $80M (FY2020) to $198M (FY2025), so a growing share of reported FCF growth is paid for in stock. The 3yr figures (24.5% owner, 23.3% reported) start from FY2022, a year depressed by Section 174 cash taxes, and overstate the run-rate."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Tyler executes the cloud migration broadly on plan but slower than the June 2026 Investor Day targets: SaaS keeps growing high-teens to ~20% while on-premises maintenance runs off 5-7% a year, so total revenue compounds ~8.5% to 2030 (~$3.5B vs the ~$3.6-3.7B implied by management's recurring target) and then fades to 3.5%. The ~1.7x revenue uplift per flipped client and hosting-cost leverage (AWS) push pre-SBC EBITDA from 27.8% to ~33% by 2030 and ~36% at maturity. Agentic-AI products contribute from 2028 but are not a separate growth leg. SBC including withholding taxes eases only from 8.5% to 7% of revenue; cash taxes normalise from the Section-174-depressed ~10% to 23%.",
    "bull": "The 2030 plan is met or beaten: peak 'flips' in 2027-2029 land at the 85% migration target, AI monetisation starting H2-2027 adds an upsell layer on the 13,000-customer installed base, and transaction revenue grows low-teens once the Texas payments contract laps. Revenue compounds ~10% to 2030 (~$3.75B), pre-SBC EBITDA reaches the mid-30s non-GAAP operating margin target (~36% EBITDA) in 2030 and 39% at maturity, and SBC drifts down to 6.5% as the stock-heavy hiring phase of the cloud build ends.",
    "bear": "State and local budgets tighten (reduced federal transfers, a recession) and multi-year migration and new-logo projects slip; maintenance attrition runs ahead of SaaS conversion, lower-cost point solutions (OpenGov, BS&A, Edmunds) cap pricing in small municipalities, and AI monetisation proves a feature rather than a revenue line. Growth decelerates to ~5-6% after FY2026, EBITDA margin expansion stalls near 31%, SBC stays ~8% of revenue, and the incremental cyber risk on sensitive court/law-enforcement data adds cost. The business remains highly cash-generative and the franchise is intact - this is a de-rating case, not an impairment case."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Tyler is a high-quality, low-churn public-sector software franchise mid-way through an on-premises-to-SaaS migration. The house valuation charges SBC plus net-share-settlement taxes in full (~8.5% of revenue) and strips interest income on the cash pile, and builds margins from components rather than carrying the FY2026 26-28% (street-basis) FCF margin forward. On that basis the value rests on whether management's 2030 margin target is delivered on time; the street (SBC-as-non-cash) basis sits much higher and is the regime most sell-side targets use.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Margin path: ~60% of value is terminal, and the terminal margin is a ~8pp expansion from today - a two-year slip in the 2030 mid-30s operating-margin target moves base value materially",
      "SBC treatment: SBC plus withholding is ~8.5% of revenue; the house/street band is wide and the price sits inside it",
      "Convertible notes ($1.4375B) are treated as debt at face; a large share-price recovery above the conversion price would turn them into dilution instead",
      "State/local budget cycle and federal-transfer cuts hit 100% of revenue at once",
      "Cash-tax normalisation: the ~10% cash rate of FY2025-H1 2026 is a Section 174 timing benefit, not a permanent rate"
    ]
  },
